<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The two targets DotGram.Web has, for its reasons: netstandard2.0 is what
		     every consumer can read, and net10.0 is what the newest runtime gets without
		     the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>

		<Description>A C#-style expression language written in .gram, compiled straight into System.Linq.Expressions trees.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>expressions;expression-tree;linq;linq-expressions;parser;csharp;dsl;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. An initializer whose braces begin `Name =` is a member initializer and nothing else, as in C#: an entry in it that is not `Name = …` is refused, with a message saying so, as C# refuses it (CS0747). An element of a collection initializer is an expression that is no assignment, also as in C#, so `{ 1, x = 2 }`, `{ b.Capacity = 2 }` and `{ x += 1 }` are refused the same way. `new List&lt;int&gt; { x = 1, 2 }` and `new List&lt;int&gt; { 1, x = 2 }` used to read `x = …` as an assignment to a variable `x` and add both, and an element could be any assignment; all of these are refused now, and that is the one change on accepted input — an assignment in parentheses, `{ (x = 1), 2 }`, is still an element. A comma may follow the last entry of an object, collection or array initializer, as in C# (`new int[] { 1, 2, }`). Empty braces read, as C# reads them, as an object initializer that sets nothing: `new List&lt;int&gt; { }`, `new Box { }`, and nested, `Items = { }`, which reads nothing, not even `Items`. An indexer may be set among members, as in C#: `new Dictionary&lt;int, int&gt; { [1] = 2 }`, `Map = { [1] = 2 }`, `[0] = { Name = … }`; since `MemberInit` has no binding for an indexer, such an initializer is built as a block of assignments, run in C#'s order (an indexer's arguments once, before what holds it is read). A member set twice in one initializer is refused, as C# refuses it (CS1912); it used to be set twice. So is an initializer of the members of a property of a value type (CS1918), which would set them on a copy; such a text used to read and then fail when compiled. An implicitly typed array, `new[] { 1, 2L }`, reads, as in C#: of the one type all its elements' types convert to, and refused where there is none (CS0826). `x with { Name = value, … }` reads, as in C#: a copy of a record class (through the clone the compiler writes for it) or of a struct, with those members set; refused for any other type (CS8858), and `with` is still a name a variable may have. `new(…)` with its type left out reads, as in C#, wherever the place it stands in says the type — a declaration, a member or element of an initializer, an argument, a `return` — and is refused where nothing does (`var x = new();`, an operator's operand). A switch with no natural type now converts to `object` as to any other target, where it was refused. A `?:` whose branches meet in no type is typed by its place too (`Box b = c ? new() : null;`), and a cast or a `throw` types a `new()`. A member's own initializer, `Items = { 1, 2 }` or `Inner = { N = 1, M = 2 }`, now reads the member once for each entry inside it, as C# does, where it read it once for all (the tree is a block of assignments then, not a `MemberInit`). Refused now as C# refuses them: a collection initializer on a type that is not `IEnumerable` (CS1922), an initializer that assigns through a setter the text cannot call or a property with none (CS0200) or a read-only field (CS0191), and an object creation that leaves a `required` member unset (CS9035). Faster: member initializers nested and never closed were read twice at every level (three quarters of a second at seventeen levels) and are read once now. A switch expression with no natural type, met as a plain operator's operand — `1 + (x switch { … })`, `(x switch { … }) == 1`, `!(x switch { … })` and the rest of the arithmetic, bitwise, shift, relational, equality and logical operators — is refused now, naming the two arms that meet in nothing, as C# refuses it (CS8506 alongside the operator's own CS0019); it used to reach the operator's own factory with no type to give it, which answered in its own words rather than the switch's, or left it to an outermost check to catch late. String concatenation is untouched: `string operator +(string, object)` is still the one place C# gives such a switch to go, boxed to `object`. `T?` reads as the nullable value type C# means by it, `System.Nullable&lt;T&gt;`, in every type position — a cast, a declaration, a generic argument, `typeof`, `default`, an array element — where it used to be refused and `System.Nullable&lt;T&gt;` had to be written whole. `is` and `as` are the one place `?` is also C#'s conditional operator, so there a bare `?` is read as nullable only where what follows it could not be the start of a new expression: `x is int ? 1 : 2` is still `(x is int) ? 1 : 2`, and `a as T ? b : c` still the ternary over `a as T`, exactly as C# reads both. A reference type's `?` is the annotation C# erases by the time anything runs, so `string?` is `string`, and a value type already nullable refuses a second mark (`int??`) as C# refuses it (CS8629). Smaller: the package no longer carries a second, internal reading of the language, compiled on the immediate carrier for the project's own tests and measurements, which nothing outside the package could call. One public type went with it: `ExpressionParser.ParserInput&lt;T&gt;`, the input the generator hands a recognizer written in C#, which it wrote here only because two readings shared one host; this language has no such recognizer, and nothing public took or returned the type. Everything else that is public is unchanged. Smaller: the internal reading of the language with its identifiers in ASCII alone, which only the tests used, is gone, and about 0.84 MB of generated source with it; nothing public changes.

Since 0.1.0. Lambdas whose parameters say no types, read where the types are known; interpolated strings, verbatim and raw; a resolution scope a host hands in, so where a name is looked for is the host's to decide — and what it means stays C#'s. Names are found in the calling assembly and what it references, as a compilation's are, and no longer in whatever the process happens to have loaded; a caller loaded into a load context of its own, a plugin, has its references found in that context, as the runtime finds them for its code. Five namespaces are there already, the set a new project gets (System, System.Collections.Generic, System.Linq, System.Text, System.Threading.Tasks); ResolutionScope.DefaultImports is the set and WithoutDefaultImports() a scope without them. `using static` brings a type's static methods, members and nested types into reach as bare names and its extension methods into the extension search, with a name the text declared winning over one it gives, as in C#; `static` is a keyword now, as it is in C#, so a text may no longer name anything it — a parameter called `static` was accepted before and is refused now. A `using` may also give a name to one type or one namespace (`using L = System.Collections.Generic.List&lt;int&gt;;`), which beats a name a `using` brings in and may not take one the global namespace has, as in C#. A full name that two referenced assemblies declare as two different types is refused where it is used, naming both assemblies, as C# refuses it (CS0433); it used to mean whichever assembly was found first. The calling assembly's own type still wins over a reference's, as in C#. Parse, TryParse and Compile are what is public, and TryParse now also answers whether without a message. Faster: an expression of a thousand terms no longer costs the square of its length, nor does a text of many blocks each declaring a name; a name that binds a parameter or a local is never looked for as a type, as in C#. The assemblies a scope covers are loaded on the first text that names a type, about 20 ms once per calling assembly and process; a text that names none loads nothing. Faster: an element of a name such as `a[0]` — nested as `a[a[a[0]]]`, a text C# accepts too — no longer reads its operand twice, once to look for a following `=` and again as a postfix operand; nesting cost exponential time before (nine seconds at twenty-four levels) and is linear now. The same double reading was in a nested array initializer's braces (`new object[] { new object[] { … x`, unclosed): allocation grew exponentially with nesting depth before and is flat now. The one outward change on accepted input, or on most refused input, is where an array initializer's own braces go wrong: `new int[] { {1} }` was refused as "'Int32[]' has no constructor taking ()" and is now refused as "Expected '}'", at the point the braces themselves fail to close. Speed against 0.1.0: the language reads more of C# (switch expressions, tuples, using static and aliases, element assignment in linear time), and an ordinary expression parses 10-30% slower than in 0.1.0 while allocating less, deeply nested parentheses about 55% slower, and an untyped lambda about 60% faster. A refused text read through the Match form is read twice to say what was expected; the bool form reads it once.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- The documentation of what is public, shipped beside the assembly. Here and not
		     in Directory.Build.targets: by the time those are read the SDK has already
		     decided where the file goes, and it goes nowhere. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, and paths normalized on CI only: both as
		     DotGram.Web has them, and the reasons are written there. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammar became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: the parser it writes acquires no runtime
		     dependency (docs/syntax.md §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(DotGramAnalyzeGenerated)' == 'true'">
		<!-- The generator's own output, analysed for dead or redundant emitted code
		     (tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers), never packed, loaded only under this
		     property (docs/development.md). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../../tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- For the consumer: the generated parser takes `ReadOnlySpan<char>`. The one
		     dependency this package has, and only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- For the compilation: types the framework lacks, emitted internal, so nothing
		     reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- The skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has restored
		     this package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README
		     says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the tests and the hand-written yardstick read and no caller should: the
		     factories the grammar builds with, the resolver, a publication written
		     `internal`. It costs nothing across the boundary since the attributes the
		     generator writes into each assembly are marked embedded, and neither sees the
		     other's. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Tests.Slow" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Benchmarks" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Handwritten" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Trace.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
